\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A paired trace-selection calculation}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{The selective-refinement comparison motivates contrasting repeatability and
coverage under a fixed retained budget
\citet{Sections 6-7; Tables 4-5, 10}{dang2026selecting}. Consider two arms from
\ref{ftip-00P8} with the same P0 boundary, model, task split, verifier, budget,
and paired assignment law. They differ only in the selector: #{a} retains the
highest teacher-forced scores and #{a'} retains a coverage-stratified subset.
Both have #{Q_{\rm hold}=\{q_1,q_2\}} and
#{\mathfrak S=\{s_1,s_2\}}. The paired assignment law gives the same held-out
task at indices 1 and 2; arm #{a'} has one additional admitted coverage run at
index 3. The receipts are}
##{
\begin{array}{c|cc|ccc}
 &q_{a,i}&s_{a,i}&A_{a,i}&I^{\rm prod}_{a,i}&Y_{a,i}\\\hline
 i=1&q_1&s_1&1&1&1\\
 i=2&q_1&s_1&1&1&0
\end{array}
\qquad
\begin{array}{c|cc|ccc}
 &q_{a',i}&s_{a',i}&A_{a',i}&I^{\rm prod}_{a',i}&Y_{a',i}\\\hline
 i=1&q_1&s_1&1&1&1\\
 i=2&q_1&s_1&1&1&1\\
 i=3&q_2&s_2&1&1&1
\end{array}
}
\p{The first two rows are the common paired indices, so
#{N_a=2}, #{N_{a'}=3}, and #{N_{\rm pair}=2}. Both arms have
#{\widehat p^{\rm prod}=1}; the third row is included in arm #{a'}'s coverage
and run-rate denominators but not in the paired contrast. Thus
#{\widehat C^{\rm task}_a=\widehat C^{\rm strat}_a=\tfrac12} and
#{\widehat C^{\rm task}_{a'}=\widehat C^{\rm strat}_{a'}=1}, while
#{\widehat\Delta_{a,a'}=-\tfrac12}. The calculation shows why a
repeatability rate alone cannot identify coverage: the two selectors have the
same productive rate but different admitted task and strategy support. This is
a finite FTIP construction using the typed receipt and pairing definitions,
not a source result.}
